"A state machine diagram defines an entity’s stable states and the events, guards, and effects that permit transitions.\n\nUse when: Specify lifecycle rules and reject invalid transitions. Explain nested or concurrent states for a reactive system.\n\nAvoid when: The behavior is best described as a sequence of one-off actions.\n\nVisual grammar: Rounded compartments denote states, with optional entry and exit behavior. Arrows carry event [guard] / effect labels. Initial, final, composite, and orthogonal-state symbols have explicit semantics.\n\nBuild: Choose the entity whose lifecycle is being modeled. List states as meaningful conditions rather than commands. Add triggering events, guards, and effects to each permitted transition. Place frequent transitions centrally and route loops around the perimeter with ample label space.\n\nCheck: Every transition has an intentional trigger or completion rule. Guards leaving a state avoid unexplained ambiguity. Nested and concurrent regions preserve their distinct semantics. Transition labels remain clear of state borders and internal text.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing an event name with the state reached afterward. Assuming a final state allows further ordinary transitions.\n\nExample: A Reservation moves from Held to Confirmed on payment approval, or to Expired when its hold timer fires.\n\nAlternatives: Activity diagram, Timing diagram."
